{"occurrence_no":"1569965","record_type":"occ","collection_no":"127499","identified_name":"Steginoporella cf. transversa","identified_rank":"species","difference":"species not entered","accepted_name":"Steginoporella","accepted_rank":"genus","accepted_no":"26237","early_interval":"Miocene","max_ma":23.04,"min_ma":5.333,"reference_no":"77754","lng":"1.500000","lat":"48.000000","cc":"FR","latlng_basis":"based on nearby landmark","latlng_precision":"degrees","geogscale":"hand sample","geogcomments":"\"The specimen was collected from a quarry at Ranville in Calvados, France, where Bathonian limestones of the Calcaire de Langrune Formation outcrop. However, bryozoans in the matrix of the specimen are of undoubted Cenozoic, probably Miocene, in age. Good preservation of both crab and bryozoans in the same matrix allows the assumption that they are contemporaneous, necessitating re-dating of H.  jurassica as probably Miocene in age. It seems likely that the piece of matrix was discarded in the Ranville quarry by a fossil collector who had previously visited a Cenozoic locality elsewhere.\" [Coordinates approximate, based on Miocene outcrop nearly Orleans and Chartres.]","environment":"carbonate indet.","primary_reference":"P. D. Taylor, G. Breton, and D. Guinot, A. De Angeli, A. Garassino. 2012. The Cenozoic age of the supposed Jurassic crab Hebertides jurassica Guinot, De Angeli & Garassino, 2007 (Crustacea, Decapoda, Brachyura). Atti della Società Italiana di Scienze Naturali e del Museo Civico di Storia Naturale di Milano 153:71-83","phylum":"Bryozoa","phylum_no":"24773","class":"Gymnolaemata","class_no":"25682","order":"Cheilostomata","order_no":"25713","family":"Steginoporellidae","family_no":"111581","genus":"Steginoporella","genus_no":"26237"},
{"occurrence_no":"1569966","record_type":"occ","collection_no":"127499","identified_name":"Membraniporella ? diadema","identified_rank":"species","difference":"species not entered","accepted_name":"Membraniporella","accepted_rank":"genus","accepted_no":"26043","early_interval":"Miocene","max_ma":23.04,"min_ma":5.333,"reference_no":"77754","lng":"1.500000","lat":"48.000000","cc":"FR","latlng_basis":"based on nearby landmark","latlng_precision":"degrees","geogscale":"hand sample","geogcomments":"\"The specimen was collected from a quarry at Ranville in Calvados, France, where Bathonian limestones of the Calcaire de Langrune Formation outcrop. However, bryozoans in the matrix of the specimen are of undoubted Cenozoic, probably Miocene, in age. Good preservation of both crab and bryozoans in the same matrix allows the assumption that they are contemporaneous, necessitating re-dating of H.  jurassica as probably Miocene in age. It seems likely that the piece of matrix was discarded in the Ranville quarry by a fossil collector who had previously visited a Cenozoic locality elsewhere.\" [Coordinates approximate, based on Miocene outcrop nearly Orleans and Chartres.]","environment":"carbonate indet.","primary_reference":"P. D. Taylor, G. Breton, and D. Guinot, A. De Angeli, A. Garassino. 2012. The Cenozoic age of the supposed Jurassic crab Hebertides jurassica Guinot, De Angeli & Garassino, 2007 (Crustacea, Decapoda, Brachyura). Atti della Società Italiana di Scienze Naturali e del Museo Civico di Storia Naturale di Milano 153:71-83","phylum":"Bryozoa","phylum_no":"24773","class":"Gymnolaemata","class_no":"25682","order":"Cheilostomata","order_no":"25713","family":"Cribrilinidae","family_no":"118664","genus":"Membraniporella","genus_no":"26043"},
