{"occurrence_no":"2071130","record_type":"occ","flags":"F","collection_no":"279963","identified_name":"Diaphus cahuzaci","identified_rank":"species","accepted_name":"Diaphus cahuzaci","accepted_rank":"species","accepted_no":"436770","early_interval":"Chattian","late_interval":"Langhian","max_ma":27.3,"min_ma":13.82,"reference_no":"78030","lng":"0.000000","lat":"44.000000","cc":"FR","state":"New Aquitaine","county":"Landes","latlng_basis":"based on nearby landmark","latlng_precision":"degrees","geogcomments":"AQUITAINE BASIN (South-West France)\nAil the sites with abundant myetophid otolith material are\nlocated in the Paleocanyon of Saubrigues (neighbourhood of\nSaubrigues, Saint-Jean de Marsacq, Saint-Martin-de-Hinx,\nSaint-Etienne-d'Orthe, Peyrehorade). From the Chattian till\nthe Langhian, the Paleocanyon has been progessively filled\nwith maris which are often very fossiliferous. The following\nnannoplankton zones have been recognised (Cahuzac et al.,\n1995; see also this paper for the précisé location of the sites):\nNP25 (Chattian): Numerous points around Saint-Etienne\nd'Orthe and Peyrehorade.\nNNI : Haubemet site at Saint-Martin-de-Hinx.\nNN2-NN3 (Lower to Middle Burdigalian): \"Les Platanes\" site\nat Saint-André-de-Seignanx.\nNN3-NN4 (Upper Burdigalian): numerous localities east and\nsouth of Saubrigues.\nNN5 (Langhian): numerous localities in the direct surroundings\nofthe village of Saubrigues; among those are the sites \"Jean Tic\"\n(presently filled in) and \"Tauziets\", which provided the rich\notolith associations described by Steurbaut in 1979 and 1984.","environment":"terrestrial indet.","primary_reference":"R. Brzobohaty and D. Nolf. 2000. Diaphus Otoliths from the European Neogene (Myctophidae, Teleostei). Bulletin de l'Institute Royal des Ciences Naturelles de Belgique 70:185-206","phylum":"Chordata","phylum_no":"33815","class":"Osteichthyes","class_no":"34881","order":"Myctophiformes","order_no":"35609","family":"Myctophidae","family_no":"82267","genus":"Diaphus","genus_no":"35614"},
